Personalized mRNA Vaccination and PD-1 Blockade in Melanoma – Interactive 3D Models

This project was created for the elective course Computational Physiology by two female medical students with a strong interest in dermatology. It shows how a personalized mRNA neoantigen vaccine and an anti-PD-1 antibody (pembrolizumab) work together against malignant melanoma.

We chose this topic because it is highly relevant at the moment. New findings and publications are changing the outlook for melanoma treatment, most notably the 2026 results of the phase 3 trial INTerpath-001. In this trial, adding the personalized mRNA vaccine intismeran autogene to pembrolizumab reduced the risk of recurrence or death in patients with resected high-risk melanoma.

We built two complementary 3D models that run directly in the browser:

Cellular model: shows a cytotoxic T cell and a melanoma cell as whole cells. It gives an overview of the process, from vaccination through PD-1 blockade to the destruction of the tumor cell, including the typical time scales.
Molecular model: zooms into the immunological synapse at the nanometer scale. It shows how the T-cell receptor recognizes a neoantigen on MHC-I, how PD-L1 binding to PD-1 shuts down T-cell signaling via the phosphatase SHP-2, how the antibody blocks PD-1, and how perforin and granzyme B finally induce apoptosis. It includes a film mode and is available in German, English, French and Spanish.

Together, the models explain why the combination works: the vaccine supplies tumor-specific T cells, and the anti-PD-1 antibody keeps them functional.

Both models are written in JavaScript using the 3D library three.js and are simplified teaching models.

Olivia Hank & Mariella Metzner
